.TH "AUDIOTRACKLIST.REMOVETRACK_EVENT" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
AudioTrackList.removetrack_event \- AudioTrackList: removetrack event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]removetrack\f[R] event is fired when a track is removed from
an \f[CR]AudioTrackList\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqremovetrack\(dq, (event) => { })
.EE
.PP
Alternatively, assign to the event handler property of the interface:
.IP
.EX
onremovetrack = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]TrackEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS Trigger
The \f[CR]removetrack\f[R] event is called whenever a track is removed
from the media element whose audio tracks are represented by the
\f[CR]AudioTrackList\f[R] object.
.PP
This event is not cancelable and does not bubble.
.SS Use cases
You can use this event to react to a new audio track becoming
unavailable.
You may want to update your UI elements to disallow for user selection
of the removed audio track, for example.
.SH EXAMPLES
Using \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] videoElement = document.querySelector(\(dqvideo\(dq);

videoElement.audioTracks.addEventListener(\(dqremovetrack\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(gaAudio track: ${event.track.label} removed\(ga);
});
.EE
.PP
Using the \f[CR]onremovetrack\f[R] event handler property (but note that
this replaces other event listeners added using
\f[CR]onremovetrack\f[R], if any):
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] videoElement = document.querySelector(\(dqvideo\(dq);

videoElement.audioTracks.onremovetrack = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(gaAudio track: ${event.track.label} removed\(ga);
};
.EE
